            <ArticleTitle>Headaches in soldiers with mild traumatic brain injury: findings and phenomenologic descriptions.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>957-65</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1111/j.1526-4610.2012.02167.x</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">The primary goal of this study was to use headache criteria-based classification for headache types described by service members.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Headache is common in soldiers returning from the wars in Afghanistan and Iraq. To date, few papers have provided detailed descriptions of these headaches.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">The first 25 patients seen by a certified headache specialist at the Traumatic Brain Injury Center at Womack Army Medical Center, Fort Bragg, NC, between August 2008 and December 2009 are reported.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Service members described a total of 55 headaches. Most, but not all, headaches began within 1 week after injury. Migraine type was most common. Aura occurred in 5 soldiers. Continuous headaches were described in 88%. Uncommon headache types including cluster type were diagnosed. Additional symptoms and service outcomes are described.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">We conclude that headaches occurring after various types of head injury, including explosions, can be assigned primary and secondary headache diagnoses using standard classifications not necessarily available to larger survey-based studies.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>© 2012 American Headache Society.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
